---
name: task-management
description: Read when recording requested work, choosing ready tasks, dispatching, tracking dependencies, or accepting delivery in Beads.
---

# Project work in Beads

Use `/usr/local/bin/mate tool beads shop -- <bd arguments>` for the project's tracker. This wrapper selects `/ws/shop/.beads/`, serializes embedded Dolt access and refreshes the viewer export. All repos in this project share that tracker. Add `--workspace /ws` before `--` when outside the workspace. The installed `bd <command> --help` is the flag reference.

1. Read `/usr/local/bin/mate tool beads shop -- list --limit 30 --json` before recording a request; reuse matching work. Create a goal with `create --title "<goal>" --type epic --json`, then work with `create --title "<work>" --type task --parent <epic-id> --description "<contract>" --json`. Use one task per repo when delivery spans repos. Beads IDs are durable; preserve them in briefs and follow-ups.
2. Select work with `ready --limit 10 --json`; inspect the candidate with `show <id> --json`. Ready work the captain asked for is yours to dispatch now, under the decision-authority and crew-dispatch rules; work nobody asked for is a proposal to the captain. For graph triage, `/usr/local/bin/mate tool beads shop --triage` runs `bv --robot-triage --brief`; the output is advice, and `bd ready`/`update --claim` establish current readiness and ownership. Interactive `mate tool beads` with no flag and bare `bv` belong to the captain's terminal.
3. Before dispatch, use `update <id> --claim --json` to claim atomically. A refused claim means someone else owns it; inspect once and choose another authorized task. Write `Beads task: <id>` into the Crew brief. After spawn, `update <id> --append-notes "Crew: <crew>; repo: <repo>; brief: crews/<crew>/brief.md"`; this is the durable execution link. Keep captain promises and questions in backlog.md; Beads owns task status and dependencies.
4. When B depends on A, `dep add <B> <A> --type blocks` makes B wait for A. Parent/child is hierarchy, not a blocking edge. For a decision or overlap with no blocking task, use `update <id> --status blocked --append-notes "Waiting for <reason>"`; when resolved, return to `open` if queued or `in_progress` if its Crew resumes. Update descriptions, priorities and labels with `update`; use `comments add <id> "<evidence or decision>"` for an audit trail.
5. On Crew handback, load `review-delivery`. Keep the task `in_progress` during review; `wait-mate`, PR open and a review-ready branch do not close it. After the ship lands or the scout's report answers its `## Deliverable`, add the delivery/acceptance evidence with `comments add`, then `close <id> --reason "<accepted outcome>"`. A rejected or discarded Crew leaves its task open/blocked unless the captain also cancels that work. Close an epic after its children have been accepted; Beads refuses closure while children or blockers remain open.

The captain opens Beads Viewer with Console `t` or `mate tool beads shop`. Its `E` tree, `b` Kanban, `g` graph and search display the same tracker. Create/update/close through the wrapper; `.beads/issues.jsonl` is a generated view, and editing it is not a Beads mutation. `mate tool beads shop --list` includes all issues; `--json` returns Beads JSON. If an error says the write may already have succeeded but viewer refresh failed, read `show <id>` and refresh with `mate tool beads shop --init`; repeating `create` can duplicate work.
